window.docContents[29].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-encodable-vertex-types":{"contents":"So far we have computed greedy colourings of graphs on ℕ. Suppose instead that we\nhave a graph G : SimpleGraph V, where V is countable. Informally we might say\n\"let the vertices of G be v₀, v₁, v₂,…\", implicitly thinking of G as a\nSimpleGraph ℕ. How can we formalize this in Lean?\n\nMathlib has the Encodable type class for constructively countable types (both\nfinite and infinite). It provides an Encodable.encode function that is an injection\nfrom V to ℕ together with a partial inverse Encodable.decode : ℕ → Option V,\nwhich satisfies decode (encode v) = some v for all v : V.\n\n#source Encodable\n\n\nIf G has an encodable vertex type then we can construct a greedy colouring of G\nby first encoding its vertices in ℕ and then applying the greedy colouring\nalgorithm to G as a graph on ℕ.\n\nOne way to build the encoded graph is to push G forward along Encodable.encode\nusing SimpleGraph.map (Maps).\n\nThe encoded graph defined via SimpleGraph.map./-- The encoded graph defined via SimpleGraph.map. -/\n@[simps!]\nabbrev encodeMap {V : Type*} [Encodable V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :\n    SimpleGraph ℕ := G.map Encodable.encode\n\n\nThis captures our informal construction above. To be precise we are not\nclaiming that G.encodeMap is isomorphic to G: in general encode need not be\nsurjective, in which case G.encodeMap will be a copy of G with some additional\nisolated vertices. However, the SimpleGraph.map construction leaves us with\nexistential quantifiers in the adjacency relation.\n\nsection encode\nopen Encodable\nvariable {V : Type*} [Encodable V]\n\nexample {a b : ℕ} {G : SimpleGraph V} : G.encodeMap.Adj a b ↔\n    a ≠ b ∧ ∃ u v, G.Adj u v ∧ encode u = a ∧ encode v = b :=\n  Iff.rfl\n\n\nThis prevents Lean from automatically deciding adjacency, even when G.Adj is\ndecidable. Since we need decidable adjacency to compute colourings, we instead build\nthe graph directly below using Encodable.decode₂, which preserves decidability.\n\nGiven [Encodable V] and a : ℕ, the function Encodable.decode₂ V a returns\nsome u such that Encodable.encode u = a, if such a u : V exists, and none\notherwise. We want a b : ℕ to be adjacent in the encoded graph iff there exist\nadjacent u v : V with Encodable.encode u = a and Encodable.encode v = b, so we\ncan define it with a match statement on Encodable.decode₂ V a and\nEncodable.decode₂ V b.\n\n#check decode₂\n\n\nThe SimpleGraph ℕ corresponding to a graph G : SimpleGraph V with encodable\nvertex type V, using Encodable.decode₂ to preserve decidability of adjacency./-- The SimpleGraph ℕ corresponding to a graph G : SimpleGraph V with encodable\nvertex type V, using Encodable.decode₂ to preserve decidability of adjacency. -/\n@[simps!]\nprotected def encode (G : SimpleGraph V) : SimpleGraph ℕ where\n    Adj := fun a b =>\n      match decode₂ V a, decode₂ V b with\n      | some u, some v => G.Adj u v\n      | _, _ => False\n    symm := ⟨fun a b => by\n      cases decode₂ V a <;> cases decode₂ V b <;> simp_all [Adj.symm]⟩\n    loopless := ⟨fun a => by cases decode₂ V a <;> simp_all⟩\n\n\nIf G is a graph on an encodable vertex type with decidable adjacency then its\nencoded graph also has decidable adjacency.\n\ninstance {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    DecidableRel (G.encode.Adj) := fun a b =>\n  match ha : decode₂ V a, hb : decode₂ V b with\n  | some u, some v => decidable_of_iff (G.Adj u v) (by rw [encode_adj, ha, hb])\n  | none, _ => isFalse (by simp [encode_adj, ha])\n  | _, none => isFalse (by simp [encode_adj, hb])\n\n\nTo transfer a greedy colouring of the graph G.encode back to a colouring of G we\nuse an embedding of G into G.encode.\n\nThe graph embedding of G into G.encode via Encodable.encode./-- The graph embedding of G into G.encode via Encodable.encode. -/\ndef Embedding.encode (G : SimpleGraph V) : G ↪g G.encode where\n  toFun := Encodable.encode\n  inj'  := encode_injective\n  map_rel_iff' := by simp\n\n\nWe would like to prove the greedy colouring bound for any graph of bounded degree on\nan Encodable, or even a plain Countable, vertex type.\n\nWe need a couple of simple lemmas. Firstly the earlier neighbourhood of an encoded\nvertex in the encoded graph is contained in the image of the neighbourhood of that\nvertex in the original graph under the encoding.\n\nlemma neighborLTFinset_encode_subset {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (v : V) : G.encode.neighborLTFinset (encode v)\n      ⊆ (G.neighborFinset v).image encode := by\n  intro m hm\n  have hadj := ((mem_neighborLTFinset ..).mp hm).2\n  match hdm : decode₂ V m with\n  | none => simp [hdm] at hadj\n  | some u =>\n    simp_all only [encodek₂, encode_adj, Finset.mem_image, mem_neighborFinset]\n    exact ⟨_, hadj, decode₂_eq_some.mp hdm⟩\n\n\nHence the degreeLT of an encoded vertex is at most the degree of the original\nvertex.\n\nlemma degreeLT_encode_le {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (v : V) : G.encode.degreeLT (encode v) ≤ G.degree v := by\n  rw [degreeLT, degree]\n  exact (card_le_card (neighborLTFinset_encode_subset v)).trans card_image_le\n\n\nUsing these lemmas we can now give the greedy (Δ + 1)-colouring of a graph with\nmaximum degree Δ on an encodable vertex type with decidable adjacency. (Note that\nwe don't actually refer to maxDegree below, since this would require a\nmathematically unnecessary Fintype V instance. Instead we assume that all vertices\nhave finite neighbourhoods using G.LocallyFinite and a bound Δ : ℕ such that\n∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ.)\n\nThe greedy Fin (Δ + 1) colouring of a graph with bounded degree Δ./-- The greedy Fin (Δ + 1) colouring of a graph with bounded degree Δ. -/\ndef greedyColoringFin {Δ : ℕ} {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ) : G.Coloring (Fin (Δ + 1)) where\n  toFun v := ⟨G.encode.greedy (encode v), by\n    grind [G.degreeLT_encode_le v, G.encode.greedy_lt (encode v)]⟩\n  map_rel' h := Fin.ne_of_val_ne ((Embedding.encode G).map_rel_iff.mpr h).greedy_ne\n\nend encode\n\n\nWe can also prove (classically) that such a colouring exists without any assumptions\nabout encodability or decidability. For this a Countable instance, together with\nthe degree bound, suffices.\n\ntheorem colorable_of_degree_le_of_countable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {Δ : ℕ}\n    [Countable V] [G.LocallyFinite] (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ) :\n    G.Colorable (Δ + 1) := by\n  classical\n  rcases nonempty_encodable V with ⟨_⟩\n  exact ⟨greedyColoringFin hdeg⟩\n\n\nLater we will use the De Bruijn–Erdős\ntheorem to show that the countable assumption is also superfluous, but for\nthis we need to understand Subgraphs.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*","header":"7.8.5. Encodable Vertex Types","id":"/Colorings/#section-encodable-vertex-types"},"/Constructions/#section-complete-bipartite-graphs":{"contents":"Given two disjoint vertex sets V and W we can form the complete bipartite graph\nwith classes V and W. This seems to involve the idea that V and W are\ndisjoint, but in Lean we are working with types not sets and so have no way of\ntalking about disjointness of two arbitrary types.\n\nLean does however have the very useful Sum type, written V ⊕ W, which plays the\nrole of the disjoint union of types V and W. Note that this is not the same as\nthe set-theoretic union: any x : V ⊕ W \"knows\" which of the two sides of the sum it\ncame from. It is either Sum.inl v for some v : V, or Sum.inr w for some\nw : W. In particular we can form the disjoint union of a type V with itself as\nV ⊕ V.\n\n#source Sum\nexample {V : Type*} (v : V) : V ⊕ V := Sum.inl v\n\n\nThis gives a natural way to define complete bipartite graphs.\n\n#source completeBipartiteGraph\n\n\nThe complete bipartite graph K_{4,5}\n\nWe introduce the notation K[s, t] for the canonical complete bipartite graph with\nparts Fin s and Fin t.\n\nThe complete bipartite graph on Fin s ⊕ Fin t is denoted K[s, t]./-- The complete bipartite graph on Fin s ⊕ Fin t is denoted K[s, t]. -/\nscoped notation \"K[\" s \", \" t \"]\" => completeBipartiteGraph (Fin s) (Fin t)\n\n\n#check K[4, 5]  -- K[4, 5] : SimpleGraph (Fin 4 ⊕ Fin 5)\n\n\nIf we try to check adjacency of two vertices in K[4, 5] we get an error. The\nactual error is suppressed in the Lean source file by the #guard_msgs command and\nonly shown in the HTML version. Delete the #guard_msgs line to see the error\nmessage in VS Code.\n\n/- error: failed to synthesize\n Decidable (K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3))\n-/\n#eval K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3)\n\n\nThis is because, as for pathGraph, there is no decidability instance for\ncompleteBipartiteGraph in Mathlib. This time, though, it is easy to provide the\nmissing instance ourselves. (Don't worry if this is unfamiliar; we will look at\ndecidability in much more detail in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.)\n\ninstance (V W : Type*) : DecidableRel (completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj :=\n    fun v w => by\n  unfold completeBipartiteGraph\n  infer_instance\n\n\nexample : K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3) := by decide\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.5. Complete Bipartite Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-complete-bipartite-graphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-darts":{"contents":"Since edges in SimpleGraph are undirected it might seem strange to introduce the\nnotion of a dart (an oriented edge). However we already noted that h : G.Adj u v\nhas a direction to it; darts simply give us a way to talk about the ordered pairs of\nadjacent vertices in a graph. These can be very useful, for example in double\ncounting arguments such as proving the handshake lemma.\n\nA dart d : G.Dart is an ordered pair of vertices together with a proof that they\nare adjacent in G. Since edges are unoriented, darts come in symmetric pairs: if\nd : G.Dart then d.symm is the opposite dart and d.edge is the edge\ncorresponding to d.\n\nsection darts\n\n#source Dart\n\n\nvariable (d : G.Dart)\n#check d.fst  -- the first vertex of the dart\n#check d.snd  -- the second vertex of the dart\n#check d.adj  -- the adjacency proof of the dart\n#check d.edge -- the edge corresponding to the dart\n#check d.symm -- the opposite dart from d.snd to d.fst\n\n\nEdges are unoriented so d and d.symm correspond to the same edge.\n\nexample : d.symm.edge = d.edge :=\n  Dart.edge_symm d\n\n\nexample : d.fst = d.symm.snd := rfl\n\nend darts\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.4. Darts","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-darts"}});